{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a methodological rule of 'critical rationalism' rather than an inductive inference; it costs us the ability to claim that the preferred theory is likely true, restricting the justification to the procedural rationality of having subjected the theory to the severest possible tests.","counterconditions":["If the agent implicitly assumes that surviving past tests increases the likelihood of surviving future tests.","If the preference is based on pragmatic utility alone without reference to truth-content.","If the definition of 'severe test' relies on inductive assumptions about background knowledge."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Distinction between logical probability (induction) and methodological preference (rational choice based on test severity). The mechanism posits that acting on the best-tested theory is the only rational way to pursue truth without assuming the future resembles the past, effectively treating corroboration as a report card of past critical efforts rather than a predictor of future success.","optional_refs":["51ef8f97a9c0ad0ac451cd6c221e1e92ab89fa2614971e29531d63b83d1904ca","d17b1621d338e4243a8aea2897eb8fc7a91873f1b78c220abd92f049f8ed6d67"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system; the strongest case for this view is that any practical reliance on past test results to guide future action constitutes a performative contradiction of the rejection of induction, costing Popper the coherence of his entire epistemology.","counterconditions":["If 'preference' can be defined purely as a psychological state without behavioral implications.","If the choice is random or arbitrary rather than based on test history.","If there exists a non-inductive principle (e.g., metaphysical faith) that justifies the preference."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Performative Contradiction Analysis. The mechanism asserts that the act of 'preferring' X over Y for future application logically entails a belief that X is more likely to succeed than Y in the future. Since this belief can only be derived from past performance, it is an inductive inference, regardless of Popper's verbal disclaimers.","optional_refs":["f759e1797c3c7358c9f05eca44bdc692d699cccb94f075e8e216f35bb555d77f","5ef493f2384fa5d512512bd3a92bd5fb87af4d88d07cebcea1440e1fca07971d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible only if reinterpreted as an evolutionary selection mechanism where theories compete and survive, making the 'preference' a description of the survival of the fittest ideas rather than a rational choice by the scientist; this costs the normative force of scientific methodology, reducing it to a naturalistic process.","counterconditions":["If scientists actively reject well-corroborated theories for ideological reasons.","If the 'environment' (tests) is not independent of the theory.","If the mechanism cannot explain why we should consciously align our actions with the 'fittest' theory."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Evolutionary Epistemology Mapping. Corroboration is recast as environmental fitness. Scientists do not 'choose' based on induction; they are the medium through which the most fit (corroborated) theories propagate. Preference is the subjective experience of objective selection.","optional_refs":["f27c7cc2aeb1e46a3ffb82cc2dc47757027cf4ea92168508d34472a5bd0fedd6","7704f0ad5f35282621ac3843d1424e60b8a8912edf24daa1c2a2bb3abdef8609"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is a pragmatic convention necessitated by the impossibility of total suspension of judgment; it is defensible as a 'rule of thumb' that acknowledges its own lack of logical foundation, costing the system its claim to provide a complete solution to the problem of induction.","counterconditions":["If the convention can be shown to have a logical basis after all.","If the 'rule of thumb' leads to systematically worse outcomes than random choice.","If Popper explicitly denied any conventional element in his methodology."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Pragmatic Conventionalism. Accepts that logically, the preference is unjustified (inductive), but argues that human agency requires a working heuristic. Popper's system survives by admitting this gap as a pragmatic necessity rather than a logical feature.","optional_refs":["0e7c744798ee87399a050abd6cd2e04b6bc10eca0bf07854be5e563dd7989460","d97262e6e412113d7242244ec05ba4dc6e8b369103ceb432746f37f06f4dd51f"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}